\begin{proof}
Let $X$ be a locally compact Hausdorff space. We show that $X$ is regular. Let $x\in X$ and let $U$ be an open neighborhood of $x$. By local compactness, there is an open neighborhood $W$ of $x$ whose closure $\overline{W}$ is compact. Since $X$ is regular Hausdorff after establishing the following separation directly, choose an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$ such that $\overline{V}\subseteq U$: indeed, apply local compactness to $U$ to obtain an open $W$ with $x\in W$ and compact $\overline{W}$, and use the Hausdorff separation of $x$ from each point of the compact set $\overline{W}\setminus U$; compactness yields finitely many such neighborhoods, whose intersection gives $V$ with $\overline{V}\subseteq U$.

Thus, for every $x\in X$ and every open neighborhood $U$ of $x$, there is an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$ with $\overline{V}\subseteq U$. Since $X$ is Hausdorff, it is $T_1$. Given a point $x$ and a closed set $F$ with $x\notin F$, take $U=X\setminus F$ and such a $V$. Then $x\in V$ and $F\cap V=\varnothing$, while $F\cap\overline{V}=\varnothing$ because $\overline{V}\subseteq U$. Hence $X$ is regular.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $X$ be locally compact and Hausdorff. We use the convention that regular means $T_1$ and point--closed-set separation. Since $X$ is Hausdorff, it is $T_1$.

Let $F\subseteq X$ be closed and let $x\notin F$. Put $U=X\setminus F$. By local compactness, there is an open neighborhood $W$ of $x$ such that $K=\overline{W}$ is compact. Set $C=K\cap F$, which is compact. For each $y\in C$, Hausdorffness gives disjoint open sets $A_y\ni x$ and $B_y\ni y$. The sets $B_y$ cover $C$, so choose $y_1,\dots,y_n$ with $C\subseteq\bigcup_i B_{y_i}$. Let $A=W\cap\bigcap_i A_{y_i}$. Then $A$ is an open neighborhood of $x$, and $\overline{A}\subseteq K$. Moreover, $\overline{A}\cap F=\varnothing$: otherwise a point of $\overline{A}\cap F$ lies in $C$ and hence in some $B_{y_i}$, while $A\subseteq A_{y_i}$ and $A_{y_i}\cap B_{y_i}=\varnothing$, a contradiction.

Thus $A$ and $X\setminus\overline{A}$ are disjoint open neighborhoods of $x$ and $F$, respectively. Therefore $X$ is regular.
\end{proof}
